WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
A PAM program cannot control what it cannot identify.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:23.320
The inventory should include privileged users, administrator roles, local accounts, directory groups, cloud roles, database administrators, network device administrators, emergency accounts, shared accounts, service accounts, API keys, automation identities, secrets, and access paths created by integrations.

3
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:28.300
Human and non-human identities both matter because both can perform privileged actions.

4
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:40.340
Inventory work should identify the owner, purpose, scope, authentication method, approval path, system dependency, and review cadence for each privileged identity or entitlement.

5
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.240
Ownership is especially important.

6
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:52.540
When no one owns an account, no one can confidently decide whether it is still needed, whether access is excessive, or whether removal would break a critical process.

7
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.260
The inventory should also distinguish identities from accounts.

8
00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:05.640
A person may have a standard user identity, one or more administrator accounts, access to group-based roles, and temporary elevation paths.

9
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:12.300
A workload may have a runtime identity, a deployment secret, a cloud role, and access to a database.

10
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.440
Treating all of those as one flat list hides risk.

11
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.580
Mapping relationships makes the program operational.

12
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:29.500
Discovery should combine directory data, endpoint data, cloud IAM data, vault data, configuration management data, ticketing records, and interviews with system owners.

13
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:36.000
Automated discovery is valuable, but human review is still needed to understand business purpose and operational dependency.

14
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:43.540
Some risky accounts exist precisely because they are old, local, shared, or outside the main identity provider.

15
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:57.500
Common failure modes include orphaned administrator accounts, former employee access, shared emergency passwords, service accounts with no owner, cloud roles with broad permissions, and local admin rights granted for convenience.

16
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.640
Corrective practice starts by ranking risk.

17
00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:08.580
Systems that can change identity, network configuration, production data, or security tooling deserve early attention.

18
00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:16.520
A healthy inventory answers who has privilege, what it can affect, why it exists, how it is protected, and when it will be reviewed.
